  "live_records": {
    "q-163558": {
      "dom_id": "step-q163558",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin is $x\\,m,$ the velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ and the acceleration is $(k-x)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where $k$ is a positive constant. If $P$ has velocity $v=4\\,m/s$ at the origin and the particle has velocity $v=2\\sqrt{6}\\,m/s$ when its displacement is $2\\,m,$ find the value of $k.$",
      "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14273713/assets/6f34d18ec0aed1f28e77a143c5be0b3d4eaf44ba08649d02517c6c6ea5bc0af4.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $(k-x)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=k-x.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=k-x.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int (k-x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=kx-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=4\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =kx-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(4)^{2} & =k(0)-\\frac{(0)^{2}}{2}+C \\\\ C & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=kx-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+8.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $v=2\\sqrt{6}\\,m/s$ when $x=2\\,m,$ we can find the value of the positive constant $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =kx-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+8 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(2\\sqrt{6})^{2} & =k(2)-\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2}+8 \\\\ 12 & =2k+6 \\\\ 2k & =6 \\\\ k & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
