    "q-163559": {
      "dom_id": "step-q163559",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$ where $x\\ge 0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin is $x\\,m,$ the velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ and the acceleration is $k(3-x)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where $k$ is a positive constant. If $P$ has velocity $v=4\\,m/s$ at the origin and has velocity $v=2\\sqrt{6}\\,m/s$ when its displacement is $2\\,m,$ find the maximum velocity of the particle.",
      "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14273713/assets/2d768dd32288c71332e81b0f72aa2652c19a7c80fed6751abea5ced383e6848f.png)\n\nThe extrema of the velocity occur at the critical points of $v.$ Since the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $k(3-x)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=k(3-x).\n$$\n\nTo find the moments where $v$ is a maximum, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=0$ and test each solution using the first derivative test.\n\nSolving $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(3-x) & =0 \\\\ 3-x & =0 \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the extremum of the velocity function occurs when $x=3\\,m.$\n\nWe now test the extreme value using the first derivative test. We summarize the necessary information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(0,3)$ | $3$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $2$ |  | $4$ |\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ | $k>0$ | $0$ | $-k<0$ |\n| $v$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nWe conclude that the velocity is maximized when $x=3\\,m.$\n\nNext, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our initial equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=k(3-x).\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int k(3-x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=k(3x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=4\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =k(3x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(4)^{2} & =k(3(0)-\\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2})+C \\\\ C & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=k(3x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})+8.\n$$\n\nAlso, since $v=2\\sqrt{6}\\,m/s$ when $x=2\\,m,$ we can find the value of the positive constant $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =k(3x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})+8 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(2\\sqrt{6})^{2} & =k(3(2)-\\frac{1}{2}{(2)}^{2})+8 \\\\ 12 & =4k+8 \\\\ 4k & =4 \\\\ k & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =1(3x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})+8 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =6x-{x}^{2}+16 \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{-{x}^{2}+6x+16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can disregard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}+6x+16}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\n{v}_{max}=v(3)=\\sqrt{-(3)^{2}+6(3)+16}=5\\,m/s\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
