    "q-163718": {
      "dom_id": "step-q163718",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin is $x\\,m,$ the velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ and the acceleration is $(kx+6)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where $k$ is a positive constant. If $P$ has velocity $v=3\\,m/s$ at the origin and the particle has velocity $v=19\\,m/s$ when its displacement is $8\\,m,$ find the value of $k.$",
      "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14273713/assets/f7468aaa11c79f4306b67a558f09cbf5d8a6a480b726e883ecb373d7e5660e37.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $(kx+6)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=kx+6.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=kx+6.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int (kx+6)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=\\frac{k{x}^{2}}{2}+6x+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=3\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{k{x}^{2}}{2}+6x+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(3)^{2} & =\\frac{k(0)^{2}}{2}+6(0)+C \\\\ C & =\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=\\frac{k{x}^{2}}{2}+6x+\\frac{9}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $v=19\\,m/s$ when $x=8\\,m,$ we can find the value of the positive constant $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{k{x}^{2}}{2}+6x+\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(19)^{2} & =\\frac{k(8)^{2}}{2}+6(8)+\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ \\frac{361}{2} & =32k+48+\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ 32k & =128 \\\\ k & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
